so some of you may have noticed that  there's a problem with YouTube today and  I'm not just talking about Oh YouTube  should listen to its creators more oh  they should do XYZ differently I am  talking about a fundamental problem with  the website itself and this isn't just  about me I'm not making this video  because I think that I'm being unfairly  treated in any way shape or form if  anything I'm in a really good position  but this is affecting a lot of people  and I think the reason for it needs to  be brought into light now if you were  paying attention to YouTube you probably  noticed that last week last Tuesday on  October 16th the website went down  worldwide for two hours it went down  which has never really happened in  YouTube before and a lot of people  wanted to know why but I have a sneaking  suspicion that I know exactly why and it  all comes down to one culprit Google+  and I'm not just using Google+ as a  scapegoat and it's easy to do that  because it's a terrible thing that was  forced on all of YouTube for no  particular reason other than Google  wanted to to give you a little history  on Google+ and why it's so deeply  ingrained in YouTube you have to go back  to 2014 when Google decided that it was  going to be mandatory for creators on  YouTube to get a Google+ account you  have to tie your YouTube channel in to  Google+ no matter what it didn't matter  if the service was good or that they  cared about natural growth they wanted  to force it down people's throats so  that they could have a Facebook  competitor now this isn't about the  morals of Google+ being integrated into  YouTube that's not what this video is  about and frankly I'm not the best to  even argue about that I'm telling you  this to emphasize how deep this really  went to see how much of YouTube had  Google+ embedded inside of it it came  down to every single programmer being  incentivized to integrate Google+ as  much as they could  they had bonuses implemented into  Google+ so that programs would be  rewarded the more they were able to  force it into YouTube's code and with  that in mind that leads into today  because before the crash on YouTube on  October 8th I believed it was announced  that Google+ was shutting down  completely because of some embarrassing  privacy leaks Google didn't want to be  exposed to regulations so they shut it  down completely  try to avoid any fanfare about it and in  doing so they have fundamentally  crippled youtube so Google+ isn't dead  yet but it started the process of  shutting down as they're shutting down  Google+ it is breaking more and more  things inside of YouTube and these  things are still broken and they're  getting worse now now it's almost a meme  at this point that people are like my  videos aren't going out to sub boxes but  now it's actually a problem across the  board a lot of youtubers are seeing a 30  to 40 percent dip in the number of  initial viewers and prolonged viewers  suggested videos aren't working properly  people aren't getting there and  notifications properly there's a lot of  different recipes that are causing  problems it's not enough that it's  completely broken but it's enough to  create a noticeable difference and an  impact on the bottom line of a lot of  youtubers just to show you how deep this  actually goes if I go into my channel  here this is this is basically my  channel the back end of my creator  studio where I manage all my videos  there are certain pages in here that  just don't actually work if I click on  these pages in here some of them take up  to a minute to load some don't load it  at all they just break and I've told  them about this and they still haven't  gotten back to me and I think the reason  is is because they don't know how to fix  it and I think that's the main problem  with YouTube right now the problem is so  big because Google+ was so deeply  integrated into YouTube they have no  idea how to separate it out at this  point so here's my guess in what  happened they pushed a code change on  Tuesday that caused all of YouTube to  crash they reverted the change and now  they're trying to analyze exactly what  happened and because it's so big they're  gonna be very slow about it  and the worst thing about it is that  this wouldn't be nearly as big a problem  if YouTube actually communicated that  there was a problem you can't just  pretend that there's nothing wrong on  the platform for people like me those  who are high up on the platform  I don't really have a problem with it  because I can weather these issues month  to month but there's a lot of creators  out there that can't that really rely on  this on a day to day basis  and as much as this is a fun job to have  as much as it's cool to entertain people  there are people that now like rely on  this rely on the consistency of it the  sad truth is that we are called partners  with YouTube but we're not partners  we're less than employees YouTube  doesn't actually care about its creators  and I'm not saying that in a very like  all the world's ending YouTube doesn't  care about us they legitimately don't  because they can't or at least they  don't have the system in place to do so  they can want it they can hope for it  but until they put the system in place  that actually gives benefit towards its  creators nothing's gonna change people  are still gonna suffer there's still  gonna be this negative attitude towards  YouTube because they rely on code and  algorithm as opposed to real people  interacting with the real people who are  making content on this platform and  until they treat the humans that are  making the content with humans that are  supporting the content it'll never work  I might be wrong about the specific  reason but that doesn't change the fact  that there is a problem with YouTube and  it doesn't change the fact that YouTube  won't tell us that there's a problem if  YouTube is gonna be a place where  creators can thrive and people can make  whatever content they want then you got  to start treating us like people all of  us if we really matter show us that we  matter and even for someone like me who  gets preferential treatment on these  things I'm still kept in the dark about  it I have to tell them about the  problems with their own platform that  they refuse to respond to me about and  if they treat me this way  what do they do to people that have less  subscribers than me it's not okay it's  really honestly truly not okay they  shoved Google+ down the throats of  everybody on this platform and now  people are suffering from it because  they don't see this platform as a place  for creativity to thrive they see it as  a tool to push their agenda and that's  fine they own it it's their right but we  help build it so anyway let me know down  the comments if you've seen other issues  with the platform if you've seen other  issues on YouTube try to keep it not so  much about the morals of it and try to  keep it down to the technical issues  that are happening  right now we've got technical problems  that need technical answers  communication is an issue and hopefully  that it'll get fixed soon but for right  now let's try to fix the problem so  thank you everybody so much for watching  and as always I'll see you in the next  one bye bye

